    "type": "question",
    "label": "32",
    "prompt": "Let $P(a,b)$ lie on $y=f(x)$.\n\nFind the new coordinates of $P$ on $y=\\frac{1-2f(1-4x)}{3}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\left(\\frac{1-a}{4},\\frac{1-2b}{3}\\right)$\n\nSet $1-4X=a$ and replace $f(a)$ by $b$.",
      "worked": "Since $f(a)=b$, the new point $(X,Y)$ satisfies\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4X&=a\\\\-4X&=a-1\\\\X&=\\frac{1-a}{4}\\\\Y&=\\frac{1-2b}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nHorizontally, dilate by factor $\\frac14$, reflect in the $y$-axis, then translate right $\\frac14$. Vertically, dilate by factor $\\frac23$, reflect in the $x$-axis, then translate up $\\frac13$.\n\nThe new coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{1-a}{4},\\frac{1-2b}{3}\\right)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 72
  },
